lua-thread-context-race: thread_context::start and its worker no longer race on the context's Lua state
version: 3
base: 9c28def3407cf3386d5c6bea932312741acb7992

This is the canonical copy of a change proposed upstream to MAME. A MAME repository
vendors it as patches/0003-lua-thread-context-race.patch and applies it after its own patches.

What it fixes: `thread_context::start` (src/frontend/mame/luaengine.cpp) hands the loaded chunk
to a detached worker that runs it on the context's one lua_State, while the main thread is still
popping the load result off that same state on its way out of start(); and the worker destroys
its result and the function after `m_busy` clears, when the next start() may already be loading
on the state. Both are data races on one Lua state, and the console plugin opens them once per
console line. The change destroys the load result before the worker exists, and has the worker
release its result and the function under the mutex before it clears m_busy.

diff --git a/src/frontend/mame/luaengine.cpp b/src/frontend/mame/luaengine.cpp
index 9ad7cea8..73e8e9ed 100644
--- a/src/frontend/mame/luaengine.cpp
+++ b/src/frontend/mame/luaengine.cpp
@@ -115,34 +115,44 @@ public:
 		if (m_busy)
 			return false;
 
-		sol::load_result res = m_state.load(scr);
-		if (!res.valid())
+		// res pops m_state's stack when destroyed, so it has to be gone before the worker starts
+		sol::protected_function func;
 		{
-			sol::error err = res;
-			luaL_error(s, err.what());
-			return false; // unreachable - luaL_error throws
+			sol::load_result res = m_state.load(scr);
+			if (!res.valid())
+			{
+				sol::error err = res;
+				luaL_error(s, err.what());
+				return false; // unreachable - luaL_error throws
+			}
+			func = res.get<sol::protected_function>();
 		}
 
 		std::thread th(
-				[this, func = res.get<sol::protected_function>()] ()
+				[this, func = std::move(func)] () mutable
 				{
-					auto ret = func();
-					std::unique_lock<std::mutex> result_lock(m_guard);
-					if (ret.valid())
+					// ret and func touch m_state when released, so both go under the lock and before m_busy clears
+					std::unique_lock<std::mutex> result_lock(m_guard, std::defer_lock);
 					{
-						auto result = ret.get<std::optional<char const *> >();
-						if (!result)
-							osd_printf_error("[LUA ERROR] in thread: return value must be string\n");
-						else if (!*result)
-							m_result.clear();
+						auto ret = func();
+						result_lock.lock();
+						if (ret.valid())
+						{
+							auto result = ret.get<std::optional<char const *> >();
+							if (!result)
+								osd_printf_error("[LUA ERROR] in thread: return value must be string\n");
+							else if (!*result)
+								m_result.clear();
+							else
+								m_result = *result;
+						}
 						else
-							m_result = *result;
-					}
-					else
-					{
-						sol::error err = ret;
-						osd_printf_error("[LUA ERROR] in thread: %s\n", err.what());
+						{
+							sol::error err = ret;
+							osd_printf_error("[LUA ERROR] in thread: %s\n", err.what());
+						}
 					}
+					func = sol::protected_function();
 					m_busy = false;
 				});
 		m_busy = true;
